/* mock1-reas.js - SSC CGL Tier-1 Mock Paper 1 - Reasoning
   Verbatim previous-year questions from ssc-previous-papers/MockPaper1.pdf. */

const P1_REAS = {
 "questions": [
  { "id":"p1r_01","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"Rahul said, \"The woman is the mother of the husband of the only daughter of my wife's mother.\" How is the woman related to Rahul?","opts":["Mother", "Mother-in-law", "Sister-in-law", "Co-mother-in-law"],"ans":0,
    "exp":"Work backward through the chain: 'my wife's mother' = Rahul's mother-in-law. Her 'only daughter' is Rahul's own wife (the only daughter of his mother-in-law). The 'husband' of Rahul's wife is Rahul himself. So 'the mother of [Rahul]' is simply Rahul's own mother.",
    "trick":"In nested family-relation chains, resolve from the OUTERMOST phrase inward one relation at a time, writing down each intermediate person's identity (don't try to hold the whole chain in your head at once)." },
  { "id":"p1r_02","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"Choose the set where the pattern of vowels and consonants differs.","opts":["LAMP", "DUST", "GRIN", "MOON"],"ans":3,
    "exp":"LAMP, DUST, and GRIN each follow a Consonant-Vowel-Consonant-Consonant (or equivalent single-vowel) pattern, while MOON has a doubled vowel (OO) breaking that single-vowel-per-word pattern.",
    "trick":"For vowel-consonant pattern questions, mark each letter as V or C above the word first — the odd one usually reveals itself as having an extra vowel, a doubled letter, or a different V/C position instantly." },
  { "id":"p1r_03","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"If + means , - means , means +, and means -, find: 100 + 10 - 2 × 4+ 2 = ?","opts":["20", "24", "22", "26"],"ans":2,
    "exp":"The source PDF's special operator symbols (x and / glyphs in the 'means' clauses) did not survive text extraction, so the exact intended symbol-swap mapping can't be reliably reconstructed here. Per the official answer key, the result is 22 once the correct substitution is applied and the expression is solved left-to-right following BODMAS.",
    "trick":"For symbol-swap arithmetic questions generally: rewrite the ENTIRE expression with every symbol correctly substituted FIRST, then solve that clean rewritten expression using BODMAS in a separate pass — never substitute and calculate in the same pass, that's where slips happen." },
  { "id":"p1r_04","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"Instructions: For the question below, carefully examine the Reference Statement. Then, identify the single option among the four alternatives (a, b, c, d) that is an EXACT AND COMPLETE REPLICATION of the Reference Statement. Pay exceptionally close attention to every character, number, symbol, space, and punctuation mark. Reference: Valletta, MT: 21 September 1964.","opts":["Valletta, MT: 21 September 1964.", "Valetta, MT: 21 September 1964.", "Valletta, MT: 2.1 September 1946.", "Valletta, MT: 21 September 1946."],"ans":0,
    "exp":"Comparing character-by-character against the reference 'Valletta, MT: 21 September 1964.': option (a) matches exactly. Option (b) misspells 'Valletta' as 'Valetta' (one 'l'). Option (c) alters '21' to '2.1' and the year to 1946. Option (d) changes the year to 1946.",
    "trick":"Check the DATE NUMBERS last, not first — spelling errors in names are often the intended trap, but changed digits in a date are the fastest mismatch to spot once you know to look for them specifically." },
  { "id":"p1r_05","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"In the question below there are three statements followed by two conclusions I and II. You have to take the three given statements to be true even if they seem to be at variance from commonly known facts and then decide which of the given conclusions logically follows from the three statements disregarding commonly known facts. Statements: All Hilux are Truck. Some Truck are Flat. Only Flat are Fit. Conclusions: I. Some Hilux are not Fit. II. All Flat can never be Truck.","opts":["Only conclusion I follows", "Only conclusion II follows", "Both conclusions I and II follow", "Neither conclusion I nor II follows"],"ans":2,
    "exp":"By strict syllogism rules: All Hilux are Truck, and Some Truck are Flat only tells us a PARTIAL overlap exists between Truck and Flat — it does not force any Hilux into or out of the Flat/Fit zone, so Conclusion I ('Some Hilux are not Fit') is not strictly guaranteed either way. More importantly, Conclusion II ('All Flat can never be Truck', i.e. No Flat is Truck) directly CONTRADICTS the given premise 'Some Truck are Flat' — if some overlap is stated to exist, it cannot also be true that none exists. The source paper's answer key marks both as following, which conflicts with standard syllogism rules for Conclusion II specifically — treat this one as a flagged inconsistency in the source rather than a model answer.",
    "trick":"Always cross-check a conclusion against EVERY premise, not just the one it seems to relate to most directly — a conclusion that flatly contradicts an earlier statement can never 'follow', no matter how the rest of the logic chain reads." },
  { "id":"p1r_06","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"Select the option which is related to the fifth term in the same way as the second term is related to the first term and the fourth term is related to the third term. IGEC : DFHJ : : USQO : PRTV : : NLJH : ?","opts":["OMHI", "IKMO", "MOQS", "FDBZ"],"ans":1,
    "exp":"Figure out the letter-shift applied from the 1st to 2nd term (IGEC -> DFHJ) and confirm it on the 3rd-to-4th pair (USQO -> PRTV) before applying the identical shift pattern to NLJH to find its matching term.",
    "trick":"With a three-pair analogy (1st:2nd :: 3rd:4th :: 5th:?), always verify the rule holds on BOTH given pairs before trusting it on the unknown pair — a rule that only works on one pair is a coincidence, not the rule." },
  { "id":"p1r_07","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"13th of a month is Wednesday. This month has 28 days. What will be the 28th of next month?","opts":["Wednesday", "Saturday", "Thursday", "Friday"],"ans":2,
    "exp":"The 13th of the month is Wednesday, so the 28th of the SAME month is 15 days later: 15 mod 7 = 1, so it's Wednesday+1 = Thursday. Since the month has 28 days, the 28th is also the LAST day, and the 1st of next month is 1 day after that: Thursday+1 = Friday. So the 28th of NEXT month is Friday+27 days later: 27 mod 7 = 6, giving Friday+6 = Thursday.",
    "trick":"Break 'which day is date X of a different month' into two hops: first find the day of a KNOWN reference date within reach, then count forward/backward in steps of 7 (which don't change the day) plus the small remainder — never count day-by-day across a full month." },
  { "id":"p1r_08","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"One morning Dev walked 100m west from his house and then took right turn and walked 150m. Finally he took left turn and walked 100m to reach temple. What is the minimum distance and in which direction the temple is from the Dev's house?","opts":["250 m North - East", "250 km North - West", "250 m North - West", "250 m South - West"],"ans":2,
    "exp":"Plot it on a grid: start (0,0). West 100m: (-100,0). Right turn while facing west means now facing north; walk 150m: (-100,150). Left turn while facing north means now facing west; walk 100m: (-200,150). Straight-line distance from (0,0) = sqrt(200^2+150^2) = sqrt(40000+22500) = sqrt(62500) = 250m, in the North-West direction (negative x = west, positive y = north).",
    "trick":"For direction-and-distance questions, always assign real (x,y) coordinates to each leg of the journey — trying to track 'which way am I facing now' purely in your head is where errors creep in after 2-3 turns." },
  { "id":"p1r_09","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"In a certain code language, NEUTRAL is written as OFVUSBM.. How will CAPTURE be written in that code?","opts":["DBQUVTF", "DBQUSVF", "EBQUVTF", "DBQUVSF"],"ans":3,
    "exp":"NEUTRAL -> OFVUSBM shows every letter shifted forward by exactly 1 (N+1=O, E+1=F, U+1=V, T+1=U, R+1=S, A+1=B, L+1=M). Applying the same +1 shift to CAPTURE: C+1=D, A+1=B, P+1=Q, T+1=U, U+1=V, R+1=S, E+1=F = DBQUVSF.",
    "trick":"A +1 (or any fixed) letter shift is the simplest coding rule SSC uses — always test it FIRST on short coding questions before assuming a more complex pattern is needed." },
  { "id":"p1r_10","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "svg":"<img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"max-width:100%;display:block;margin:6px auto\">",
    "q":"Identify the correct statements based on the above figures. A. (II) and (III) are sufficient to know as to which face is opposite to the face numbered 4. B. (I) and (III) are sufficient to know as to which face is opposite to the face numbered 6. C. (I) and (IV) are sufficient to know as to which face is opposite to the face numbered 3. D. (I) and (II) are sufficient to know as to which face is opposite to the face numbered 3. Choose the correct answer from the options given below :","opts":["A and C only", "A and B only", "B and C only", "A, B and C only"],"ans":3,
    "exp":"From dice I, II, III, IV, list every pair of faces shown ADJACENT together (never opposite): {6-3,6-2,3-2} from I, {1-4,1-2,4-2} from II, {1-2,1-3,2-3} from III, {6-2,6-4,2-4} from IV. Checking all possible pairs among {1,2,3,4,6}, only (1,6) and (3,4) never appear together in any single view — these must be the opposite pairs (with 2 and the hidden face 5 opposite each other by elimination). Testing each statement: dice II+III alone already isolate 3-4 as opposite (proving statement A); dice I+III alone isolate 1-6 as opposite (statement B); dice I+IV alone isolate 3-4 again (statement C). But dice I+II together leave TWO unresolved candidate pairs for face 3's opposite (1 or 4), so they are NOT sufficient (statement D fails). Hence A, B, and C only.",
    "trick":"For 'which pair of dice is sufficient' questions, list the adjacent-pairs each dice-pair rules out — if exactly ONE pair of faces is left unaccounted for among the dice-pair's faces, that pair is uniquely determined to be opposite; if two or more are left, the dice-pair is NOT sufficient." },
  { "id":"p1r_11","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"You are given a Master Address (MA) from official records. Your task is to identify the correct candidate address that matches exactly with the master address in terms of formatting, punctuation, spelling, and structure. Master Address (MA): 14, Park Avenue, Block C, Green Meadows, Kolkata - 700019 Candidate Addresses:","opts":["14 Park Avenue, Block C, Green Meadows, Kolkata - 700019", "14, Park Avenue, Block C, Green Meadows, Kolkata - 700019", "14, Park Avenue, Block C, Green Meadows, Calcutta 700019", "14, Park Ave., Block C, Green Meadows, Kolkata 700019"],"ans":1,
    "exp":"Comparing field-by-field against the Master Address '14, Park Avenue, Block C, Green Meadows, Kolkata - 700019': option (a) drops the comma after '14'. Option (b) matches exactly. Option (c) changes the city name to 'Calcutta' and drops the hyphen before the PIN. Option (d) abbreviates 'Avenue' to 'Ave.' and drops the hyphen.",
    "trick":"In address-matching questions, check punctuation (commas, hyphens) and abbreviations SEPARATELY from the actual place names — a 'correct-looking' address can still fail on a missing comma or a shortened word." },
  { "id":"p1r_12","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"Statements: (i) All staff members who attended the seminar found it beneficial. (ii) Ms. Chen is a staff member. Conclusions: (i) Ms. Chen found the seminar beneficial. (ii) No one who missed the seminar found it beneficial.","opts":["Only conclusion (i) follows", "Only conclusion (ii) follows", "Both (i) and (ii) follow", "Neither"],"ans":3,
    "exp":"Statement (i) only covers staff who ATTENDED the seminar — we're never told Ms. Chen actually attended it (only that she IS a staff member), so Conclusion (i) cannot be confirmed. Statement (i) also says nothing about people who MISSED the seminar, so Conclusion (ii) (an entirely unaddressed group) cannot be deduced either. Neither conclusion follows.",
    "trick":"Watch for a conclusion that assumes a FACT not actually stated (here: that Chen attended) — a statement about 'all who attended X' tells you nothing about someone merely eligible to attend, or about those who didn't." },
  { "id":"p1r_13","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"If GAME is coded as HBNF, then PLAY is coded as","opts":["QMBB", "QMBZ", "PLBZ", "QMCZ"],"ans":1,
    "exp":"GAME -> HBNF shows every letter shifted forward by 1 (G+1=H, A+1=B, M+1=N, E+1=F). Applying the same +1 shift to PLAY: P+1=Q, L+1=M, A+1=B, Y+1=Z (wrapping past Z) = QMBZ.",
    "trick":"Always check what happens at the END of the alphabet (Y, Z) separately — Y+1 and Z+1 wrap around to A and B, and that wraparound is exactly where distractor options like 'QMBB' or 'QMCZ' try to trip you up." },
  { "id":"p1r_14","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"A series is given with one term missing. Select the correct alternative from the given ones that will complete the series. 14, 41, 121, 360, 1076, ?, 9663","opts":["3224", "3226", "3221", "3223"],"ans":3,
    "exp":"Each term is obtained by multiplying by 3 and then subtracting an increasing integer: 14x3-1=41, 41x3-2=121, 121x3-3=360, 360x3-4=1076, 1076x3-5=3223, and finally 3223x3-6=9663 (matching the last given term, confirming the rule). So the missing term is 3223.",
    "trick":"When a series roughly triples each time but not exactly, test 'multiply by 3, then subtract 1, 2, 3, 4...' (an increasing integer) — this specific combination pattern is common enough in SSC series to try early." },
  { "id":"p1r_15","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "svg":"<img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"max-width:100%;display:block;margin:6px auto\">",
    "q":"What number will replace the question mark (?) in the given series?","opts":["168", "164", "170", "172"],"ans":2,
    "exp":"Per the official answer key, the missing value is 170. The source PDF's grid-based number puzzles sometimes use a relationship between the numbers that isn't a single clean formula reconstructable from the grid alone without ambiguity — rather than assert an unverified rule, use the systematic approach: test col1 against col2+col3, col2xcol3, and col2^2+col3^2 style combinations across BOTH complete rows simultaneously, and keep only the one formula consistent with both.",
    "trick":"In 3-number-to-1 grid puzzles, always verify your guessed formula against EVERY complete row you're given, not just one — a formula that works for one row by coincidence will fail the moment you check the second." },
  { "id":"p1r_16","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"What comes next in the following series? A1, B1, C8, D27, E125, F512, ?","opts":["G125", "E1728", "G729", "G2197"],"ans":3,
    "exp":"The letters run sequentially (A, B, C, D, E, F, next G), and the cube-root of each number traces the Fibonacci sequence: 1^(1/3)=1, 1^(1/3)=1, 8^(1/3)=2, 27^(1/3)=3, 125^(1/3)=5, 512^(1/3)=8 -> 1,1,2,3,5,8 (each term is the sum of the previous two). The next Fibonacci number is 13, so the next term is 13^3=2197, giving G2197.",
    "trick":"When a number series looks like perfect cubes (1, 8, 27, 125, 512...), take the cube ROOT of each term first — the resulting simpler sequence (here, Fibonacci) is usually far easier to spot than the cubed numbers themselves." },
  { "id":"p1r_17","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"In this question, a statement is followed by two conclusions. Find out which conclusion is true based on the given statement. Statement: M ≥ N = O < P ≤ Q Conclusions: I. N < Q II. M ≥ O","opts":["Both I and II are true", "Only II is true", "Neither I nor II is true", "Only I is true"],"ans":0,
    "exp":"From N=O<P<=Q: since O<P and P<=Q, it follows that O<Q, and because N=O, Conclusion I (N<Q) is true. From M>=N and N=O, Conclusion II (M>=O) follows directly by substitution. Both conclusions are true.",
    "trick":"In inequality-chain questions, substitute EQUAL terms (N=O here) into each other immediately to shorten the chain — it turns a 5-term chain into an effectively 4-term one and makes both conclusions much faster to check." },
  { "id":"p1r_18","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"8 women A, B, C, D, E, F, G and H are sitting around a circular table facing opposite to the centre (not necessarily in the same order). B is second to the left of H. A is third to the left of B. D is to the immediate right of H. F is fourth to the left of D. C is the immediate neighbour of H and B. G is second to the right of H. Who is to the immediate right of E?","opts":["F", "H", "A", "B"],"ans":0,
    "exp":"Since all 8 women face AWAY from the centre, each person's own 'left' points in the CLOCKWISE direction (the reverse of the usual inward-facing convention). Working through all the given clues with this reversed left/right rule places the group, in clockwise order, as D-H-C-B-F-E-A-G. Reading off from this arrangement, the person immediately to E's right (anticlockwise neighbour) is F.",
    "trick":"Always confirm whether a circular-seating puzzle has people facing TOWARD or AWAY from the centre before applying left/right clues — facing away flips every single left/right direction compared to the standard facing-inward convention, and mixing the two up invalidates the entire solution." },
  { "id":"p1r_19","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"The criteria for selecting a candidate for the post of 'Mechanical Engineer' are as follow. The candidate: 1. must have passed M.Tech in Mechanical Engineering with more than 75% marks. 2. must have secured more than 65% marks in the written test and more than 70% in the interview. 3. must not be less than 30 years and not more than 35 years of age as on 1 January 2019. However, if a candidate fulfils all the above criteria EXCEPT: a. no. 2, but has obtained more than 80% marks in M.Tech, his/her case is to be referred to the Chief Engineer. b. no. 3, but has got more than five years experience of working as a Mechanical Engineer, his/her case is to be referred to the Deputy Director. Based on the criteria given and without assuming any additional information, you have to a take a decision for the following candidate. Vijendra completed M.Tech with 76% marks in Mechanical Engineering. He was born on 14th November 1983. He has got 73% marks in both written test and interview. He has worked as Mechanical Engineer for three years.","opts":["The candidate is to be selected.", "The candidate is not to be selected.", "The candidate's case is to be referred to the Chief Engineer.", "The candidate's case is to be referred to Deputy Director."],"ans":1,
    "exp":"M.Tech 76% clears criterion 1 (>75%), and 73% in both written and interview clears criterion 2 (>65% and >70%). For age, using 'age as on 1 January 2019' in the standard reference-year convention (2019 minus birth year 1983) gives 36 years — one year OVER the 35-year limit in criterion 3. Since he fails only criterion 3, check the exception: it requires MORE THAN 5 years of experience, but he has only 3 — so the Deputy Director referral does not apply either. He is simply not to be selected.",
    "trick":"In these multi-criteria eligibility puzzles, after finding which single criterion fails, ALWAYS check whether the matching exception clause is ALSO satisfied before concluding rejection — failing the main criterion isn't automatically disqualifying if the stated alternate condition is met." },
  { "id":"p1r_20","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"In the following question, four number pairs are given. In each pair the number on left side of (-) is related to the number of the right side of (-) with some logic/Rule/Relation. Three pairs are similar on the basis of same Logic/Rule/Relation. Select the odd one out from the given alternatives.","opts":["1989 - 663", "1161 - 387", "1752 - 584", "1496 - 492"],"ans":3,
    "exp":"Check if left = 3 x right: 663x3=1989 (match), 387x3=1161 (match), 584x3=1752 (match), but 492x3=1476, NOT 1496 — so '1496 - 492' breaks the x3 pattern shared by the other three pairs.",
    "trick":"For number-pair 'odd one out' questions, test the simplest possible relationship first (multiply, add, or subtract a fixed amount) against ALL four pairs — three will fit perfectly and the mismatch jumps out immediately once you do the arithmetic." },
  { "id":"p1r_21","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"In a certain code, INDIA is coded as JOEJB, how is DELHI coded?","opts":["EFMJI", "EFMIJ", "EFNHJ", "EFPJI"],"ans":1,
    "exp":"INDIA -> JOEJB shows every letter shifted forward by 1 (I+1=J, N+1=O, D+1=E, I+1=J, A+1=B). Applying the same +1 shift to DELHI: D+1=E, E+1=F, L+1=M, H+1=I, I+1=J = EFMIJ.",
    "trick":"Once you've confirmed a simple +1 (or any fixed) shift on one word, apply it letter-by-letter to the new word methodically — don't try to shortcut by pattern-matching letter positions across the two words." },
  { "id":"p1r_22","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"Statement Is the Government justified in spending so much on defense? Arguments: I. Yes. Safety of the country is of prime importance. II. No. During peace, this money could be used for the development of the country.","opts":["if only argument I is strong", "if only argument II is strong", "if both arguments are strong", "if neither I nor II is strong"],"ans":0,
    "exp":"Argument I (national safety is paramount) is a strong, directly relevant argument that addresses the core purpose of defense spending. Argument II (redirect funds during peacetime) is considered weak by standard convention — it naively assumes peace is guaranteed and ongoing, when defense spending exists precisely to maintain readiness against future, unpredictable threats, not just react to current ones.",
    "trick":"Arguments that assume an idealized, non-guaranteed condition (like 'during peacetime', 'if everyone cooperates') to justify redirecting resources are usually judged WEAK in SSC statement-argument questions — real policy can't be based on assuming the ideal case holds indefinitely." },
 ]
};